Make a module that does not compile fail the build

py_compile.compile() without doraise reports to stderr and returns, so the loop
walks on and the build carries a package whose byte code is missing. The stderr
of the interpreter is thrown away on top of that, leaving nothing in the log
either. A merge that went into enigma2 with its conflict markers still in place
was packaged this way, and every receiver flashed with it stopped at "No module
named" and restarted, over and over.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [embedded specific]

Signed-off-by: WXbet <57314510+WXbet@users.noreply.github.com>

--- a/lib/py-compile
+++ b/lib/py-compile
@@ -196,8 +196,8 @@
         continue
     sys.stdout.write(file + ' ')
     sys.stdout.flush()
-    py_compile.compile(filepath, destpath(filepath), path)
-sys.stdout.write('\n')" "$@" 2>/dev/null || exit $?
+    py_compile.compile(filepath, destpath(filepath), path, doraise=True)
+sys.stdout.write('\n')" "$@" || exit $?
 
 # Then byte compile w/more optimization.
 # Only do this for Python 3.5+, see https://bugs.gnu.org/38043 for background.
@@ -220,8 +220,8 @@
         continue
     sys.stdout.write(file + ' ')
     sys.stdout.flush()
-    py_compile.compile(filepath, importlib.util.cache_from_source(filepath), path)
-sys.stdout.write('\n')" "$@" 2>/dev/null || exit $?
+    py_compile.compile(filepath, importlib.util.cache_from_source(filepath), path, doraise=True)
+sys.stdout.write('\n')" "$@" || exit $?
   ;;
 esac
 
